21:26.447 --> 21:30.557
<i>That year, winter came as it always does.</i>

21:31.167 --> 21:34.177
<i>And eventually, summer followed.</i>

21:35.367 --> 21:38.577
<i>On the steppe, the grass 
sprouts anew once more,</i>

21:39.007 --> 21:42.507
<i>and the sheep graze upon the soft buds.</i>

22:08.817 --> 22:11.177
<i>Perfectly unchanging.</i>

22:12.897 --> 22:14.667
<i>The steppe, same as ever.</i>

22:20.727 --> 22:22.137
<i>But is it really?</i>

22:23.167 --> 22:25.297
Good day to you, Great Khatun.

22:25.827 --> 22:27.907
Fine weather we're having.

22:28.537 --> 22:32.757
It's as though it embodies 
the growing prosperity of the empire.

22:34.187 --> 22:36.067
Yes, I would agree.

22:37.057 --> 22:39.377
Second Empress Töregene.

22:40.787 --> 22:42.147
And her lady-in-waiting...

22:43.207 --> 22:44.507
Fatima.

22:51.687 --> 22:57.497
<i>That year, Emperor Ögedei 
appointed a governor to Persia,</i>

22:58.187 --> 23:04.737
<i>and Sitara's homeland fell under 
the permanent rule of the Mongol Empire.</i>

23:06.197 --> 23:10.457
<i>However, the dispute over this position</i>

23:11.087 --> 23:16.917
<i>would eventually turn into a huge storm 
that would shake the empire to its core.</i>

23:17.967 --> 23:21.107
<i>But no one knew this yet.</i>

